   FFFFCC kkeeyy ggeenneerraattiioonn ppaarraammeetteerrss
       The  following  key  generation  types  are  available  for DSA and DHX
       algorithms:

       "type" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__TTYYPPEE) <UTF8 string>
           Sets the type of parameter generation. The shared valid values are:

           "fips186_4"
               The current standard.

           "fips186_2"
               The old standard that should only be used for legacy purposes.

           "default"
               This can choose one of "fips186_4" or "fips186_2" depending  on
               other parameters set for parameter generation.

       "pbits" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__PPBBIITTSS) <unsigned integer>
           Sets the size (in bits) of the prime ’p’.

       "qbits" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__QQBBIITTSS) <unsigned integer>
           Sets the size (in bits) of the prime ’q’.

           For  "fips186_4"  this  can  be either 224 or 256.  For "fips186_2"
           this has a size of 160.

       "digest" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__DDIIGGEESSTT)  <UTF8 string>
           Sets the Digest algorithm to be used as part of the Key  Generation
           Function  associated  with the given Key Generation _c_t_x.  This must
           also be set for key validation.

       "properties" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__DDIIGGEESSTT__PPRROOPPSS) <UTF8 string>
           Sets properties to be used upon look up of the  implementation  for
           the  selected  Digest  algorithm  for  the  Key Generation Function
           associated with the given key generation _c_t_x. This may also be  set
           for key validation.

       "seed" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__SSEEEEDD) <octet string>
           For  "fips186_4"  or "fips186_2" generation this sets the _s_e_e_d data
           to use instead of generating a random seed internally. This  should
           be  used  for testing purposes only. This will either produce fixed
           values for the generated parameters OR it will fail if the seed did
           not generate valid primes.

       "gindex" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__GGIINNDDEEXX) <integer>
       "pcounter" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__PPCCOOUUNNTTEERR) <integer>
       "hindex" (OOSSSSLL__PPKKEEYY__PPAARRAAMM__FFFFCC__HH) <integer>
           These types are described above.

